WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Unstable transfer can engulf two stellar cores in

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.760
one gaseous envelope. Drag transfers orbital energy into

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.680
that gas while the cores spiral closer. If

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.320
the envelope escapes soon enough, a close binary

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.020
can survive. If it remains, the continuing inspiral

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.660
can merge the cores. The shared envelope does

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.240
not guarantee one outcome.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.180
A merger can release orbital energy and produce

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.960
a luminous red nova. In the observed example,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.380
an eclipsing binary’s period shortened before the outburst.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.460
That changing precursor supports the merger interpretation. Despite

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.680
the similar name, a luminous red nova is

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.180
different from hydrogen igniting on a white dwarf

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.660
in a classical nova.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.610
Mass gain or a merger can give a

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.290
star more mass and mix fresh hydrogen toward

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.670
its core. In an old cluster, the result

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.690
may lie above and to the blue side

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:53.490
of the ordinary turnoff: a blue straggler. Its

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.610
evolutionary state looks younger, but elapsed time has

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.370
not reversed. One such star does not reset

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.630
the cluster’s age.
